//! The daemon's whole configuration, in one versioned JSON document.
//!
//! ## Why JSON, and why generated
//!
//! dnsmasq's configuration is a line-oriented dialect with about two hundred
//! directives, of which this network uses twenty-three. All twenty-three are
//! generated by Nix (`machines/gw/lib/network-tables.nix`), and the two faults
//! that cost the most here were **shape** faults in that dialect rather than
//! policy faults:
//!
//!   * a `dhcp-host` line that named one address twice made dnsmasq **exit**
//!     (`duplicate dhcp-host IP address … FAILED to start up`, 2026-10-21), and
//!     the never-give-up policy turned that into an endless restart loop on the
//!     box that answers DNS and DHCP for the building;
//!   * `local=/int.leighhack.org/` without the suffix on every hosts line
//!     answered **nothing** for the FQDN every monitor asks (2026-10-17).
//!
//! Neither is expressible here: there are no lines, an address is a value
//! rather than the left-hand side of a comma list, and a name is either in the
//! zone or it is not. The same class of mistake — two devices claiming one MAC,
//! a tag that matches no interface, a reservation inside a dynamic range —
//! is a **finding with a sentence**, collected at build time by `netd check`.
//!
//! ## Where the boundary is
//!
//! `common/netd/mk-config.nix` emits values Nix already owns: the VLAN table,
//! the reservation table, the front-door names, the zone, the upstreams. This
//! crate decides what each one *means*, and nothing about the network is
//! restated on the Nix side to make the JSON look nicer. That is the "absorb
//! Nix config" half: adding a field means touching the schema here and the
//! renderer there in one commit, and the compiler is the thing that remembers.
//!
//! Files: `schema.rs` (what the fields are), `parse.rs` (reading it, with a
//! path in every error), `validate.rs` (the rules about relationships between
//! fields), `tests.rs`. Split because one 1300-line file is not a review.

mod parse;
mod schema;
mod validate;

// The module's public surface. `cargo check` does not see the tests, so the
// items only the tests read would otherwise be warnings here — and a re-export
// list is exactly the kind of thing nobody prunes.
#[allow(unused_imports)]
pub use schema::{
    canonical_name, client_id_hex, client_id_parse, hex_parse, mac_parse, mac_to_string, Api,
    ClientId, Conditional, Config, Dhcp, DhcpOption, Dns, Mac, Named, Netboot, OptionValue, Pool,
    Ra, RaIface, RaNet, Reservation, Soa, V4Net,
};
use std::path::Path;

impl Config {
    /// Read and validate a document from disk. The error is the file's own
    /// message plus every finding, which is what `netd check` prints and what a
    /// failed build shows.
    pub fn load(path: &Path) -> Result<Config, String> {
        let text = std::fs::read_to_string(path).map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", path.display()))?;
        Config::parse(&text).map_err(|e| format!("{}:\n{e}", path.display()))
    }
}

